 ## Overview
-GS-IR is an inverse rendering approach based on 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) that leverages forward mapping volume rendering to achieve photorealistic novel view synthesis and relighting results [S1]. It estimates scene geometry, surface material, and environment illumination from multi-view images captured under unknown lighting conditions [S1]. Unlike NeRF-based inverse rendering methods that use implicit neural representations and ray tracing (backward mapping), which suffer from low expressive power and high computational complexity, GS-IR uses 3D Gaussians and forward mapping splatting for real-time rendering efficiency [S1]. It addresses the specific limitations of 3DGS in inverse rendering: the lack of native plausible normals and the inability of forward mapping to trace occlusion for indirect lighting [S1].
+GS-IR is an inverse rendering approach based on 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) that leverages forward mapping volume rendering to achieve photorealistic novel view synthesis and relighting results [S1]. It estimates scene geometry, surface material, and environment illumination from multi-view images captured under unknown lighting conditions [S1]. Unlike NeRF-based inverse rendering methods that use implicit neural representations and ray tracing (backward mapping), which suffer from low expressive power and high computational complexity, GS-IR uses 3D Gaussians and forward mapping splatting for real-time rendering efficiency [S1]. It addresses the specific limitations of 3DGS in inverse rendering: the lack of native plausible normals and the inability of forward mapping to trace occlusion for indirect lighting [S1]. It differs from its sibling [Inner 3D Gaussian Splatting](15745-inner-3d-gaussian-splatting.md) in its explicit integration of a PBR pipeline and environment map optimization for relighting.
 
